    title: 'Q-Sort Analysis',
    about: 'how Q methodology finds shared viewpoints in people’s sorts, and which statements set those viewpoints apart.',
    intro: [
      'Q methodology studies how people see a topic. Each person places a set of statements on a grid, by how much they agree with each one. The result is called a Q sort. Stephenson (1935) proposed turning factor analysis around so that it correlates people instead of tests. People who sort the statements in a similar way then end up on the same factor, which stands for a shared viewpoint (Brown, 1980). In health research, Q has been used most often to study the training of health workers and nursing practice (Churruca et al., 2021).',
      'This page runs the classical analysis on a small study that Brown (1980) used to teach the method. Nine people each described their own country by sorting 33 statements about its values. Five came from the United States, and one each from Japan, Canada, England and France. They sorted from most uncharacteristic to most characteristic of their country, shown here as most disagree to most agree. The tabs pick a viewpoint. Clicking a person compares their own sort with that viewpoint, and clicking a statement shows where each viewpoint places it.',
    ],
    explanation: [
      'Every sort fills the same forced grid, with two statements at each end and more toward the middle. Sorters often start with three rough piles and then fill the grid from both ends inward (Brown, 1980, 1993). The fixed shape makes people weigh the statements against each other, yet it barely changes the results. The middle column holds statements a person feels little about, and also ones that seem unclear or hard to place (Brown, 1980).',
      'The analysis begins by correlating every pair of sorts. The correlation <i>r</i> is 1 for two identical sorts and −1 for two sorts that mirror each other. Click US4 and the list under the map shows its <i>r</i> with the other eight. US3 is most alike at .40, and JP5 is least alike at −.56. With 33 statements, an <i>r</i> beyond about ±.34 is unlikely to arise by chance alone (Brown, 1980).',
      'Principal components analysis then sums up these correlations with a few factors. Varimax rotation turns the factors so that the loadings tend to be either large or close to zero (Kaiser, 1958). Rotation leaves the correlations between sorts unchanged (Brown, 1980). This page keeps three factors and follows the R package qmethod. Its results match those published for these data (Zabala, 2014; Zabala & Pascual, 2016). Brown (1980) found much the same three groups with centroid factors rotated by hand, so his loadings differ from these.',
      'A loading is roughly the correlation between a person’s sort and a viewpoint’s sort (Brown, 1980). On the map, the position across shows the loading on the chosen viewpoint. The height shows the loading on the other two combined. A sort counts toward a viewpoint when two things hold (Zabala, 2014). Its loading must pass the cut-off, and its square must be larger than its other squared loadings added together. Those sorts fall in the shaded areas. The cut-off starts at .34, the line for <i>p</i> &lt; .05. Brown (1980) used the stricter line of .45, for <i>p</i> &lt; .01, and at that line FR9 drops out of viewpoint 3.',
      'A negative loading means a sort is close to the mirror image of the viewpoint. JP5 loads −.83 on viewpoint 1. Statements that US3 and US4 placed near most agree, JP5 tended to place near most disagree. Brown (1980) kept such a sort and flipped it before adding it to the viewpoint’s sort. He also advised that when several sorts sit at the reversed end, that end be read as a viewpoint of its own.',
      'Each viewpoint’s sort is a weighted average of the sorts that count for it. Sorts with higher loadings get much larger weights. The averages are turned into <i>z</i>-scores and put back into the same grid (Brown, 1980). The result shows how a person who fully shared that viewpoint would sort the statements (Zabala et al., 2018). Click US3 and the grid takes the colors of US3’s own sort, in near-clean bands. Click JP5 and the bands run the other way.',
      'Two viewpoints differ on a statement when their <i>z</i>-scores for it are far enough apart. At the start, a gap of more than 0.77 is significant at <i>p</i> &lt; .05 (Brown, 1980; Zabala & Pascual, 2016). S16 is about close ties to Britain as the Mother Country. Viewpoint 3, held by the Canadian and English sorters, places it at +4, and the other two place it at −3 and −4. This makes S16 a distinguishing statement for viewpoint 3. A statement on which no pair of viewpoints differs is called a consensus statement.',
      'This test assumes that each person’s sort has a reliability of .80 (Brown, 1980). As a result, every statement on a viewpoint gets the same standard error, and the test cannot show which placements are less certain (Zabala & Pascual, 2016).',
      'Q shows which viewpoints exist among the people studied. It cannot say how common each one is (Brown, 1980; Zabala et al., 2018). Three of the nine sorters sharing a viewpoint says nothing about how many people in their countries share it. Counting that needs a separate survey built on the Q results (Churruca et al., 2021).',
    ],
    references: [
      'Brown, S. R. (1980). <i>Political subjectivity: Applications of Q methodology in political science</i>. Yale University Press.',
      'Brown, S. R. (1993). A primer on Q methodology. <i>Operant Subjectivity, 16</i>(3/4), 91–138. https://doi.org/10.22488/okstate.93.100504',
      'Churruca, K., Ludlow, K., Wu, W., Gibbons, K., Nguyen, H. M., Ellis, L. A., & Braithwaite, J. (2021). A scoping review of Q-methodology in healthcare research. <i>BMC Medical Research Methodology, 21</i>, Article 125. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12874-021-01309-7',
      'Kaiser, H. F. (1958). The varimax criterion for analytic rotation in factor analysis. <i>Psychometrika, 23</i>(3), 187–200. https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02289233',
      'Stephenson, W. (1935). Technique of factor analysis. <i>Nature, 136</i>(3434), 297. https://doi.org/10.1038/136297b0',
      'Zabala, A. (2014). qmethod: A package to explore human perspectives using Q methodology. <i>The R Journal, 6</i>(2), 163–173. https://doi.org/10.32614/RJ-2014-032',
      'Zabala, A., & Pascual, U. (2016). Bootstrapping Q methodology to improve the understanding of human perspectives. <i>PLOS ONE, 11</i>(2), Article e0148087. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0148087',
      'Zabala, A., Sandbrook, C., & Mukherjee, N. (2018). When and how to use Q methodology to understand perspectives in conservation research. <i>Conservation Biology, 32</i>(5), 1185–1194. https://doi.org/10.1111/cobi.13123',
    ],
